WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to Building
Global Secure Access.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:06.975
This is Chapter 4: Microsoft
traffic, and change plans.

3
00:00:06.975 --> 00:00:10.058
The Microsoft traffic
profile is the prerequisite

4
00:00:10.058 --> 00:00:12.925
for everything identity-aware
the service adds.

5
00:00:12.925 --> 00:00:15.216
Turning it on is also the first write,

6
00:00:15.216 --> 00:00:19.186
so this chapter builds the plan
every later write goes through:

7
00:00:19.186 --> 00:00:23.462
a file a second person approves by
its hash before anything changes.

8
00:00:23.462 --> 00:00:25.976
The chapter is a draft awaiting review,

9
00:00:25.976 --> 00:00:29.870
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:34.770
Why a plan, now?

11
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:36.585
Because source IP restoration,

12
00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:41.330
the compliant network check and universal
tenant restrictions all depend on the

13
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:42.936
Microsoft traffic profile,

14
00:00:42.936 --> 00:00:46.495
and switching it on is the first
time the kit changes anything.

15
00:00:46.495 --> 00:00:48.270
So the goal has two halves.

16
00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:52.085
Show that a ring's Microsoft
365 and Entra ID traffic

17
00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:56.432
goes through the service,
carrying its real source address.

18
00:00:56.432 --> 00:01:00.946
And change the tenant only through a plan
file whose hash a reviewer approved.

19
00:01:00.946 --> 00:01:04.670
It's for the developers, the
platform owner who runs the changes,

20
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.890
and the change board's reviewer.

21
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.015
Here's what Learn gives.

22
00:01:12.015 --> 00:01:16.142
The Microsoft traffic
profile acquires Microsoft

23
00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:20.077
365 and Entra ID traffic,
and only that profile can.

24
00:01:20.077 --> 00:01:22.897
Learn's Graph tutorial
finds a profile by its

25
00:01:22.897 --> 00:01:25.640
traffic type and sets
its state with a patch.

26
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.702
Assigning users and groups is a
portal step on the pages read.

27
00:01:29.702 --> 00:01:34.565
Signaling is source IP restoration and
the signal Conditional Access needs.

28
00:01:34.565 --> 00:01:37.912
It's on by default for newer
tenants, and Learn cautions that

29
00:01:37.912 --> 00:01:41.190
turning it off while policies
depend on it can block users.

30
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:45.628
The test compares a SharePoint sign-in's
address with the client on and off.

31
00:01:45.628 --> 00:01:50.030
And Learn treats enabling a
new profile as a major change.

32
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.005
What does it leave to you?

33
00:01:55.005 --> 00:01:57.454
Each change is one patch typed by hand,

34
00:01:57.454 --> 00:02:01.640
and a change board can't approve
a command that isn't written down,

35
00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.905
so the kit writes the plan as a file,
with every request and its hash.

36
00:02:05.905 --> 00:02:07.882
The assignment is a portal step,

37
00:02:07.882 --> 00:02:11.305
so the plan lists it as a manual
line with the click path.

38
00:02:11.305 --> 00:02:14.633
A plan could turn signaling
off in the wrong order,

39
00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:18.855
so plans turn it on first and only
the rollback turns it off, last.

40
00:02:18.855 --> 00:02:23.318
A daily percentage has no pass or
fail, so the check sums the week.

41
00:02:23.318 --> 00:02:26.069
And nothing ties a
profile to its decision,

42
00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:29.330
so a profile with no
approved decision fails.

43
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.080
Here's the plan.

44
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.381
It reads a desired-state file: the
three profiles' state, signaling,

45
00:02:38.381 --> 00:02:42.443
tagging, and the guide's Conditional
Access policies as files.

46
00:02:42.443 --> 00:02:47.570
It compares them with a collection and
writes the requests that close the gap,

47
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:51.176
in a safe order: signaling,
then profiles, then policies,

48
00:02:51.176 --> 00:02:54.542
then tagging, with the
assignments as manual steps.

49
00:02:54.542 --> 00:02:55.554
Four guards.

50
00:02:55.554 --> 00:03:00.405
New policies are report-only, and
enforcing one needs an explicit switch.

51
00:03:00.405 --> 00:03:04.392
A policy that doesn't exclude the
emergency group isn't planned.

52
00:03:04.392 --> 00:03:07.781
Tagging waits for a blocking
tenant restrictions default.

53
00:03:07.781 --> 00:03:10.968
And a drifted policy is
brought back to its file.

54
00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:13.579
Nothing applies without the approved hash,

55
00:03:13.579 --> 00:03:16.480
a live collection and a delegated session.

56
00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:21.155
Who does what?

57
00:03:21.155 --> 00:03:25.418
The platform owner writes the
desired state, plans and applies.

58
00:03:25.418 --> 00:03:28.073
A second person, the
change board's reviewer,

59
00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:31.392
approves the plan's hash, and
the change record keeps it.

60
00:03:31.392 --> 00:03:34.105
The security monitor
reads the sign-in share.

61
00:03:34.105 --> 00:03:39.680
For permissions, the reads need Network
Access Read All and access to the workspace.

62
00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.312
The writes are Network Access
Read Write All for the profiles,

63
00:03:43.312 --> 00:03:48.155
signaling and tagging, and Policy Read
Write Conditional Access for the policies.

64
00:03:48.155 --> 00:03:51.344
The portal assignment needs
Application Administrator,

65
00:03:51.344 --> 00:03:55.690
activated through privileged identity
management for the window only.

66
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.252
Lab 1.

67
00:04:00.252 --> 00:04:04.866
Steps 1 and 2 list the reads and writes
and collect the whole pilot tenant,

68
00:04:04.866 --> 00:04:07.865
because the plan compares
against everything.

69
00:04:07.865 --> 00:04:10.227
Step 3 runs the traffic checks.

70
00:04:10.227 --> 00:04:12.185
The profile and signaling are on,

71
00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:16.390
and the internet and private profiles
each have an approved decision.

72
00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:20.790
But only two thousand five hundred
fifty-five of two thousand eight hundred

73
00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.862
sign-ins, ninety-one percent,
went through the service,

74
00:04:23.862 --> 00:04:26.027
under the ninety-five percent target.

75
00:04:26.027 --> 00:04:31.470
Step 4 reads the guide's query day
by day: rising, but not there yet.

76
00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.307
Step 5 plans the pilot
from its desired state.

77
00:04:38.307 --> 00:04:39.658
Two Graph steps.

78
00:04:39.658 --> 00:04:41.540
The internet profile goes off,

79
00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:45.832
because the pilot's desired state
hasn't adopted Internet Access yet.

80
00:04:45.832 --> 00:04:49.016
And the administrative
RDP policy goes back

81
00:04:49.016 --> 00:04:51.770
to its reviewed file; Chapter 6 says why.

82
00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.145
Two portal steps assign the pilot group.

83
00:04:54.145 --> 00:04:57.683
Then the plan's hash, and
dry run, nothing changed.

84
00:04:57.683 --> 00:05:00.469
Step 6 reads the plan's
first step in full,

85
00:05:00.469 --> 00:05:05.113
as the reviewer does before approving
the hash: the call, the method,

86
00:05:05.113 --> 00:05:09.450
the address on graph dot
microsoft dot com, and the reason.

87
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.825
Step 7 tries to apply the plan in
fixture mode, with a made-up hash.

88
00:05:17.825 --> 00:05:20.571
It's refused before the hash is even read:

89
00:05:20.571 --> 00:05:25.812
apply runs only with a live invoker,
and fixture mode never changes anything.

90
00:05:25.812 --> 00:05:30.474
The tests also show the other refusals:
a live session with the wrong hash is

91
00:05:30.474 --> 00:05:35.212
refused, and the right hash sends the
steps in order through the Graph runner.

92
00:05:35.212 --> 00:05:39.749
Step 8 runs this chapter's
tests: nine passed, none failed.

93
00:05:39.749 --> 00:05:41.940
Then delete the output folder.

94
00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.644
Lab 2 uses a test tenant with Entra ID P1,

95
00:05:48.644 --> 00:05:53.628
a pilot group of fictional users
and a test device with the client.

96
00:05:53.628 --> 00:05:56.458
Sign in, collect, and
plan from a desired file

97
00:05:56.458 --> 00:05:59.840
that only turns on Microsoft
traffic and signaling.

98
00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.652
Send the plan and its hash to the reviewer,
then apply it with the approved hash.

99
00:06:04.652 --> 00:06:08.915
Assign the pilot group in the portal,
as the plan's manual line says.

100
00:06:08.915 --> 00:06:12.609
Validate with Learn's test,
comparing the sign-in address with

101
00:06:12.609 --> 00:06:16.303
the client on and off, then the
guide's query and the kit's checks.

102
00:06:16.303 --> 00:06:19.358
Back it out in the guide's
order: remove the group,

103
00:06:19.358 --> 00:06:22.178
disable the profile,
and leave signaling on.

104
00:06:22.178 --> 00:06:24.530
The authors haven't run this lab.

105
00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:31.905
The profile and signaling
support remote access control.

106
00:06:31.905 --> 00:06:34.718
The sign-in share, with
the real source address,

107
00:06:34.718 --> 00:06:38.493
supports the content of audit
records and system monitoring.

108
00:06:38.493 --> 00:06:42.185
And every plan, approved by
its hash and applied in order,

109
00:06:42.185 --> 00:06:46.279
supports configuration change
control and impact analysis.

110
00:06:46.279 --> 00:06:49.681
The guide reads this
step as TIC 3.0's remote

111
00:06:49.681 --> 00:06:52.342
user pattern to a
sanctioned cloud service.

112
00:06:52.342 --> 00:06:54.506
The assessor decides the controls.

113
00:06:54.506 --> 00:06:59.490
The dangers: a plan nobody read, Internet
Access turned on before its decision,

114
00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.605
and signaling turned off
while policies need it.

115
00:07:02.605 --> 00:07:06.018
You're done when the profile and
signaling are on for the ring,

116
00:07:06.018 --> 00:07:10.318
the week's share meets the target, and
every change came from an approved plan.

117
00:07:10.318 --> 00:07:14.840
Next, Chapter 5: the compliant
network and tenant restrictions.
